  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Early this afternoon, northern MI is \nsandwiched between upper ridging over the southeast states and \nclosed low pressure centered over far northern Quebec. Last night's \ncold front now situated to our south...draped from NE eastward \nthrough central IL into far southern MI/Ohio Valley. Meanwhile, \n~1037mb sfc high pressure centered near the Manitoba/Ontario border \nwill drift southeast across Ontario through Tuesday while a mid-\nlevel shortwave treks across the northern Plains/upper MS Valley. As \nthat wave continues to depart into Ontario/Quebec on Wednesday, the \nsurface boundary to our south will lift north as a warm front \nsetting the stage for another round of above normal temperatures \nacross northern MI. Next chance for rain holds off until late \nWednesday night - Thursday as another area of low pressure treks by \nto our north, dragging a cold front across the area locally. \nAdditional shower chances may present themselves at times over the \nHalloween weekend, but with a wide envelope of solutions possible at \nthis time with respect to coverage/timing.\n\nForecast Details: Not a whole lot of sensible weather to speak of \nthrough midweek. The only possible blemish to this arrives late \ntonight into Tuesday morning, primarily across far northwest lower \ninto the eastern U.P., as that upstream wave passing by to our west \nmay be just enough to kick off a few isentropically driven showers \nlocally, although the vast majority will remain well off to our \nwest. Otherwise, cooler/near-normal temperatures expected Tuesday \nwith highs ranging from the upper 40s far north and through the 50s \nover northern lower.\n\nAnother warm up for Wednesday under partial sunshine. Latest trends \nfavor high temperatures spanning the 60s area-wide with a few lower \n70s possible. This places at least a handful of climate sites within \na few degrees of daily records (ANJ, PLN, GLR, HTL).  While Thursday \n- Sunday high temperatures will be a touch cooler than Wednesday, \nabove normal temperatures are expected to continue -- generally \nranging from the mid-upper 50s far north and through the 60s south.\n\nShower chances return late Wednesday night into Thursday as a cold \nfront crosses northern MI. Coverage/intensity of these showers \nlooking less and less impressive with time, but still will be the \ntime frame that features the highest precip chances over the next \nweek. Looking toward Halloween, above normal temperatures mentioned \nabove expected. Shower chances may return late in the day, but lots \nof spatial/temporal uncertainty continue.",
